<!--    kenneth: we specify the geom pair explicitly, and filter out any body-pair cause we don't set contype/conaffinity to 1. -->
<!--    condim=3 is Regular frictional contact, opposing slip in the tangent plane.-->
<!--    friction: default to  “1 1 0.005 0.0001 0.0001”-->
<!--      NOTE: when explicitly specify the friction/condim in pair, and the properties of the individual geoms are not used.  -->
<!--      friction vector's two coefficients are tangential friction coefficients of "geom1 geom2", can be different
    to generate anisotropic tangential friction, e.g., skating. -->

<!--      1. Start with realistic values for standard materials
You can use published data for common materials as a starting point.
Rubber on dry concrete: Up to 1.0.
Steel on steel (dry): ~0.6.
Steel on steel (greasy): ~0.1.
Polymer on polymer: 0.2 to 0.4.
Ceramics: Very low (0.02 to 0.2).
Lubricated surfaces: Generally below 0.1.
-->
<!--  The magnitude of the combined friction force components will be bounded by the friction coefficient (mu)
 times the normal force N, i.e.:  F <= mu * N
 The solver's optimization ensures this constraint is respected. -->

